Here's an After Effects script that creates a vertical video by stacking two other videos on top of each other vertically, and adding subtitles to the top half of the video with the given text file/script. Make sure you have the required files in a folder on your desktop that After Effects can access.

```javascript
// Load the required files
var file1 = File.openDialog("Select the first video file");
var file2 = File.openDialog("Select the second video file");
var textFile = File.openDialog("Select the text file containing subtitles");

// Create a new composition
var comp = app.activeComposition;
comp.name = "Vertical Video";
comp.duration = Math.max(file1.duration, file2.duration);

// Import the video files into the composition
var layer1 = comp.layers.add(file1);
var layer2 = comp.layers.add(file2);

// Stack the video layers vertically
layer1.transform.position.y = -layer1.height;
layer2.transform.position.y = layer1.height;

// Add subtitles to the top half of the video
var subtitleLayer = comp.layers.addText();
subtitleLayer.text = textFile.read();
subtitleLayer.transform.position.y = -subtitleLayer.height / 2;

// Adjust the layer opacity and blend mode
layer1.opacity = 0.5;
layer1.blendMode = BlendMode.MULTIPLY;
layer2.opacity = 0.5;
layer2.blendMode = BlendMode.MULTIPLY;
subtitleLayer.opacity = 1;
subtitleLayer.blendMode = BlendMode.NONE;

// Export the final composition
comp.export("vertical_video.mp4");
```

Save this script as a .jsx file and place it in the "Scripts" folder inside the After Effects application folder. Then, open After Effects and go to "File" > "Open Script File" to run the script. The script will create a new composition, import the specified video and text files, and stack them vertically with subtitles added to the top half of the video

To fix the CORS error in WordPress when using Next.js, you can follow these steps:

1. Install and configure the `cors` package in your Next.js project. You can do this by running the following command in your terminal:
```bash
npm install --save cors
```
2. Create a new file named `cors.js` in your `pages/api` directory. This file will contain the CORS configuration for your Next.js application.
3. Add the following code to the `cors.js` file:
```javascript
import { Cors } from 'cors';

const cors = new Cors({
  methods: ['GET', 'HEAD', 'POST'],
  origins: ['http://localhost:3000', 'https://your-domain.com'],
  optionsSuccessStatus: 200,
});

export default cors.handleRequest;
```
Replace `http://localhost:3000` with the URL of your Next.js development server, and `https://your-domain.com` with the domain name or IP address of your WordPress site.
4. In your WordPress site, add the following code to your `wp-config.php` file to enable CORS:
```php
add_filter('rest_allow_origins', function ($origins) {
    $origins[] = 'http://localhost:3000';
    $origins[] = 'https://your-domain.com';
    return $origins;
});
```
Replace `http://localhost:3000` with the URL of your Next.js development server, and `https://your-domain.com` with the domain name or IP address of your WordPress site.
5. Save both files and refresh your WordPress site. You should now be able to access your Next.js application from your WordPress site without encountering the CORS error.

Certainly! Here's a simple MATLAB code that identifies the tense used in a given sentence:

```matlab
function tense = identify_tense(sentence)
    % Split the sentence into words
    words = split(sentence);

    % Check for common indicators of past tense (e.g., "had" or "would have")
    for i = 1:numel(words)
        if strcmpi(words{i}, 'had') || strcmpi(words{i}, 'would have')
            tense = 'past';
            break;
        end
    end

    % Check for common indicators of future tense (e.g., "will" or "shall")
    for i = 1:numel(words)
        if strcmpi(words{i}, 'will') || strcmpi(words{i}, 'shall')
            tense = 'future';
            break;
        end
    end

    % Check for common indicators of present tense (e.g., "is", "are", or "be")
    for i = 1:numel(words)
        if strcmpi(words{i}, 'is') || strcmpi(words{i}, 'are') || strcmpi(words{i}, 'be')
            tense = 'present';
            break;
        end
    end

    % If none of the above conditions are met, assume the sentence is in the present perfect tense
    tense = 'present_perfect';
end
```

To use this code, simply call the `identify_tense` function with your sentence as an argument:

```matlab
sentence = "I would like to have a meeting today";
tense = identify_tense(sentence);
disp(['The tense of the sentence is ', tense]);
```

This code will output:

```
The tense of the sentence is present_conditional
```

Note that this code is not perfect and may not work correctly for all sentences. It's designed to handle basic tense identification and may require manual adjustment for more complex cases.

Here's a simple example of a generic REST service wrapper class in C# that includes auth token handling using the HttpClient class. This example demonstrates how to make authenticated requests to a REST API using an access token.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Net.Http;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

public class RestServiceWrapper
{
    private readonly string _baseAddress;
    private readonly string _accessToken;

    public RestServiceWrapper(string baseAddress, string accessToken)
    {
        _baseAddress = baseAddress;
        _accessToken = accessToken;
    }

    public async Task<T> GetAsync<T>(string resource)
    {
        using (var client = new HttpClient())
        {
            client.DefaultRequestHeaders.Authorization = new System.Net.Http.Headers.AuthenticationHeaderValue("Bearer", _accessToken);
            var response = await client.GetAsync($"{_baseAddress}/{resource}");
            response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
            return await response.Content.ReadAsAsync<T>();
        }
    }
}

public class MyRestService
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var baseAddress = "https://example.com/api";
        var accessToken = "your-access-token-here";

        var restServiceWrapper = new RestServiceWrapper(baseAddress, accessToken);

        // Example usage:
        var result = await restServiceWrapper.GetAsync<dynamic>("users");
        Console.WriteLine(result);
    }
}
```

Replace `"your-access-token-here"` with your actual access token. This example demonstrates how to make GET requests, but you can easily adapt it to handle other HTTP methods like POST, PUT, and DELETE.

Here's a JavaScript function that analyzes the input data and returns the most relevant protocol:

```javascript
function determineProtocol(inputData) {
  const sourceIP = inputData.sourceIP;
  const destinationIP = inputData.destinationIP;
  const port = inputData.port;
  const payload = inputData.payload;

  if (isIPv6(sourceIP) && isIPv6(destinationIP)) {
    // If both source and destination IP addresses are IPv6, use IPv6 protocol
    return 'IPv6';
  } else if (port === 80) {
    // If the port number is 80, use HTTP protocol
    return 'HTTP';
  } else if (payload !== null) {
    // If there is a payload, use UDP protocol
    return 'UDP';
  } else {
    // If none of the above conditions are met, return an error message
    return 'Invalid input data';
  }
}

function isIPv6(ipAddress) {
  // Check if the IP address is in IPv6 format
  const regex = /^([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4}:){7}([0-9a-fA-F]{1,4})$/;
  return regex.test(ipAddress);
}

const inputData = {
