Here's an example Google Apps Script that searches for a place name in a column and stores the latitude and longitude in other columns:

```javascript
function searchPlace() {
  var sheet = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSheet();
  var dataRange = sheet.getRange("A2:A"); // Assuming your data starts in cell A2
  var placeName = dataRange.getValue();

  var geocoder = new google.maps.Geocoder();
  geocoder.geocode({ 'address': placeName }, function(results, status) {
    if (status == 'OK') {
      var latitude = results[0].geometry.location.lat();
      var longitude = results[0].geometry.location.lng();

      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue(latitude); // Assuming you want to store the latitude in column B
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue(longitude); // Assuming you want to store the longitude in column C
    } else {
      sheet.getRange("B2").setValue("Error"); // Assuming you want to store an error message in column B
      sheet.getRange("C2").setValue("Error"); // Assuming you want to store an error message in column C
    }
  });
}
```

To use this script, follow these steps:

1. Open your Google Sheet.
2. Click on "Extensions" > "Apps Script."
3. Copy and paste the script into the script editor.
4. Save the script.
5. Go back to your Google Sheet.
6. Click on the button with the three dots next to it and select "Run function" > "searchPlace."
7. The script will search for the place name in the first cell of column A and store the latitude and longitude in cells B2 and C2, respectively.

Note: You'll need to enable the Google Maps API in your Google Cloud Platform project to use this script. You can find instructions on how to do this here: https://developers.google.com/maps/documentation/geocoding/introduction#enable-the-geocoding-api

