```javascript
function smoothLinearMap(x, minX, maxX, minY, maxY) {
  const t = (x - minX) / (maxX - minX);
  const result = minY + t * (maxY - minY);

  // Add some smoothness at the edges
  if (t <= 0.1 || t >= 0.9) {
    return Math.pow(result, 0.5);
  }

  return result;
}
```

This function takes in the input value `x`, as well as the minimum and maximum values for both the input and output ranges. It then calculates a value `t` between 0 and 1 based on the input value, and uses this value to linearly interpolate between the minimum and maximum output values.

To add some smoothness at the edges, we check if `t` is less than or equal to 0.1 or greater than or equal to 0.9. If it is, we square the result, which will give us a smoother transition.

You can call this function with your desired input and output ranges like this:

```javascript
const inputRange = [0, 1];
const outputRange = [0, 1];

