Certainly! Here's an example of natural selection using MATLAB code:

```matlab
% Define the population size and initial traits
population_size = 100;
initial_traits = randi([0 1], population_size, 1); % 0 represents a disadvantageous trait, 1 represents an advantageous trait

% Define the fitness function
fitness_function = @(traits) sum(traits); % The fitness function is based on the sum of the traits, where a higher value indicates a more favorable trait

% Simulate natural selection for 100 generations
for i = 1:100
    % Evaluate the fitness of each individual
    fitness_values = fitness_function(initial_traits);

    % Select individuals for reproduction based on their fitness
    selected_individuals = initial_traits(sort(fitness_values, 'descend'));

    % Create offspring by combining the traits of selected individuals
    offspring_traits = zeros(population_size, 1);
    for j = 1:population_size
        offspring_traits(j) = selected_individuals(randi([1 population_size]));
    end

    % Update the population with offspring
    initial_traits = offspring_traits;
end

% Plot the evolution of the population over time
figure;
plot(1:100, mean(initial_traits));
xlabel('Generation');
ylabel('Average Trait Value');
title('Evolution of Population under Natural Selection');
```

In this example, we simulate natural selection over 100 generations, where the fitness of each individual is based on the sum of their traits. The individuals with the highest fitness are selected for reproduction, and their offspring inherit a combination of their parents' traits. Over time, the population evolves to have a higher average trait value, indicating that the advantageous trait has become more prevalent.

